ayed queen to e3  very simply centralizing the queen  reinforcing the knights also  pressuring e-file a bit  and here carwana decides to take  immediately on f3  which at first i thought was a little  bit surprising given that black  had the bishop hair and uh very quickly  just gives it up  but then i realized that lightsword  bishop for black doesn't really have a  great place especially if h3 is coming  black probably wants to trade before  white can land and move like 95.  so after takes takes  e6  and uh here i think it's safe to say  that we've officially left opening and  the players are entering the middle game  and this is where white can start trying  to formulate some plans  i think a lot of players here would very  simply just improve the rook rook 81  but magnus decides to start with the  move h4 which of course is going for h5  and very much in the style of stockfish  or lila just pushing the h pawn trying  to grab space and potentially attack on  the king side  so fabiano wants none of this he plays  h5 himself and then magnus plays a very  solid move g3  and uh from white's perspective it makes  sense to keep the pawns on the dark  squares because white has lights for  bishop and usually in cases like this  the way to maximize square control is to  have the pawns control the dark squares  have the bishop control light squares  even though it's still a very solid  position for both sides  i do already kind of like the the piece  harmony that white has going on so the  game continues rook fe-8 rook 81 proving  the final piece in the position  and now black plays a6 probably going  for some queen side expansion would be  very logical to go for b5 and eventually  b4 especially combined with the fiancato  dark square bishop  and here we reach a point where  white pretty much has everything fully  optimized it seems like  and it's a question  what to do next  and i think a position like this can  really separate  a an amateur player from a strong grand  master especially someone like magnus  carlsen  and  i really like the move that magnus  played here because he identified  essentially his worst place piece in the  position  and very quickly turned his worst piece  into his best piece  so i'll go ahead and show the move is  knight to d2  uh which looks a little bit passive it  is retreating but it's not about where  the knight  is gone it's about where the knight is  going  and uh if we just play one more move  pawn b5 we're gonna see white's really  nice idea and this idea it's positional  but it's also tactical it's knight to e4  and the knight can't be taken thanks to  the x-ray vision along the d-file  if black were to take the knight then  white would be winning material  uh because the queen is hit the knights  hit and if the queen moves then white  can take and hit both rooks  so knight e4 was uh was very clever from  magnus and i'm sure he saw this uh back  when his knight was on b3 it's to play a  knight e4 force a queen to move and then  find a very nice square on g5 here we  see that white is putting a lot of  pressure  on the king side from relatively early  on in the middle game so here the game  continues rook 88 bishop t2  uh nice move unleashing the rook and uh  just getting the bishop to a slightly  preferable square  and here fabiano makes the first clear  mistake of the game what he probably  should have done is just waited and been  patient and played a move like bishop f6  and just see what white is gonna do next  it's very possible this could have  prompted white to repeat after 94 bishop  g7 knight g5 perhaps magnus would uh  would try and play on for more of an  advantage  but the move that carwana played here  was pawn to b4  and this is a double-edged move on one  hand it does uh try and weaken white's  queen side but on the other hand it  allows the bishop to find a very nice  square  on a4 and this is what magnus played to  shish kebab all of these pieces  on the light squares  and as we're gonna see b4 just did more  damage to black's position  than it did to white's position  so here black took on c3 white took back  and then queen c8 so getting off the  diagonal  unfortunately for black the play was a  little bit slow and passive  and this gave magnus the perfect  opportunity to strike  and in this position he came up with a  really beautiful move that i think a lot  of players just wouldn't consider here  is a move pawn to f5  and usually when there's a square that's  controlled by two of the opponent pawns  and only supported by your queen then  such a move is off limits but here white  takes advantage of the fact that  uh whichever pawn takes there's going to  be things hanging  if the g pawn takes then each pawn will  be undefended and if the e pawn takes  then d5 will be undefended  and already black is under serious  serious pressure  in the game  fabiano took on f5 with the g-pawn  and i was really impressed with magnus's  next move because it looks like  queen jigs h5 is a no-brainer  but uh then black would play rook e7 and  it's not entirely clear how light can  follow through with attack queen h7 is  met with king f8  and perhaps there's still some survival  chances left for black  so instead of going into this line  magnus started with a move pawn c4  and the difference here is that the  queen stays on f3 which essentially pins  upon to the knight on c6 as white you  can very safely take the pawn whenever  you feel like it so there's really no  rush to take on h5  and essentially what magnus is doing is  creating multiple weaknesses for black  to deal with and usually when there's  multiple targets to attack then it's  easier to uh to win material and come  out on top  so fabiano here played knight d4  which is an attempt to muddy the waters  and try and make things a bit  complicated because now the queen is  attacked by the knights but at the same  time the rook is attacked on e8s  and i think uh this move may have been a  bit of de